WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.405
The train passed rapidly across the state of Iowa. During the night it crossed the Mississippi River

2
00:00:07.406 --> 00:00:13.497
and entered Illinois. At four o'clock the next day, the travelers reached Chicago. Nine hundred miles separated Chicago

3
00:00:13.498 --> 00:00:18.652
from New York, but trains were plentiful along this route. Mr. Fogg led his companions

4
00:00:18.653 --> 00:00:23.262
from one train to another without pause as they traversed Indiana, Ohio,

5
00:00:23.263 --> 00:00:26.341
Pennsylvania, and New Jersey in a flash. Their

6
00:00:26.342 --> 00:00:31.311
last train followed the Hudson River into New York. Late on the evening of December

7
00:00:31.312 --> 00:00:36.979
11, it stopped at the station, right beside the pier where the ocean-going

8
00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:45.417
steamers docked. But the steamer for Liverpool had left port three-quarters of an hour before! Passepartout was crushed. Mr. Fogg, however, merely said, "We

9
00:00:45.418 --> 00:00:51.736
will decide what is best tomorrow." They crossed the Hudson River on a ferryboat and took a carriage to

10
00:00:51.737 --> 00:01:00.232
a hotel on Broadway. The next morning, after instructing Passepartout and Aouda to be ready at a moment's notice, Mr. Fogg left the hotel. It was 7:00

11
00:01:00.233 --> 00:01:04.285
a.m. on December 12. Until 8:45 p.m. on December 21—Mr.

12
00:01:04.286 --> 00:01:07.301
Fogg's last minute for completing his journey—there remained

13
00:01:07.302 --> 00:01:12.884
9 days, 13 hours, and 45 minutes. Mr. Fogg went to the banks of the Hudson to

14
00:01:12.885 --> 00:01:18.566
scout among the anchored ships. They were mostly sailing boats, which would be too slow.

15
00:01:18.567 --> 00:01:22.295
He was ready to give up hope when he spotted a

16
00:01:22.296 --> 00:01:28.684
trading vessel, its stack puffing a cloud of smoke, ready to depart. He called out for the captain, who presented

17
00:01:28.685 --> 00:01:32.655
himself immediately. He was a man around fifty, a sort

18
00:01:32.656 --> 00:01:40.009
of sea-wolf with big eyes and a growling voice. His name was Andrew Speedy. "Is your ship a fast

19
00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:47.365
one?" asked Mr. Fogg. "Yes. Its name is the Henrietta , and it is well known," said the captain. "Will you

20
00:01:47.366 --> 00:01:52.295
carry me and three other passengers to Liverpool?" asked Mr. Fogg. "No. I

21
00:01:52.296 --> 00:01:58.002
am setting out for Bordeaux, France," replied the captain. "I am willing to pay any sum of money," said Mr.

22
00:01:58.003 --> 00:02:02.510
Fogg. "It makes no difference," said Captain Speedy. "My

23
00:02:02.511 --> 00:02:05.652
destination is Bordeaux." "I will buy your ship,"

24
00:02:05.653 --> 00:02:11.553
insisted Mr. Fogg. "No," refused the captain flatly. Phileas Fogg did not betray his disappointment, but

25
00:02:11.554 --> 00:02:19.318
the situation was serious. Up to this point, money had removed all difficulties. He thought hard. He must cross the Atlantic

26
00:02:19.319 --> 00:02:22.425
one way or another. "Well, will you

27
00:02:22.426 --> 00:02:29.542
carry me to Bordeaux?" he asked. "No," answered Captain Speedy. "I offer you two thousand dollars," said Fogg.

28
00:02:29.543 --> 00:02:36.141
"Per passenger?" asked the captain, becoming interested. "Yes. Two thousand dollars per passenger," agreed Mr. Fogg. Captain Speedy scratched his

29
00:02:36.142 --> 00:02:39.170
head. He had eight thousand dollars to

30
00:02:39.171 --> 00:02:43.789
gain without changing his route! "I start in an hour," he

31
00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:49.761
said simply. "We will be onboard," said Mr. Fogg, and true to his word, he, Aouda, Passepartout,

32
00:02:49.762 --> 00:02:54.319
and Mr. Fix were standing on the deck when the Henrietta lifted its anchor.

33
00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:59.404
The next day as the ship steamed eastward, a man mounted the bridge to check the ship's

34
00:02:59.405 --> 00:03:02.900
position. He unrolled the navigation charts and gazed out

35
00:03:02.901 --> 00:03:08.229
over the sea. Who was in command of the Henrietta ? It was

36
00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:13.535
none other than Phileas Fogg! What had happened was simple. Phileas Fogg wanted to go

37
00:03:13.536 --> 00:03:18.861
to Liverpool. Captain Speedy would not take him. So Mr. Fogg had bribed

38
00:03:18.862 --> 00:03:23.752
all the sailors, and they had switched their loyalty to him. Captain Speedy was locked in his

39
00:03:23.753 --> 00:03:31.704
cabin screaming loudly. Passepartout thought Mr. Fogg's maneuver was simply glorious. It was clear, as he watched Mr. Fogg

40
00:03:31.705 --> 00:03:35.405
manage the craft, that Mr. Fogg had been a sailor. Therefore

41
00:03:35.406 --> 00:03:39.459
if the sea did not become too stormy, if

42
00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:42.595
the wind did not turn against them, if no

43
00:03:42.596 --> 00:03:47.901
accident happened to the boat or its machinery, the Henrietta would arrive in

44
00:03:47.902 --> 00:03:52.737
Liverpool in nine days, on December 21. The first days of the crossing

45
00:03:52.738 --> 00:03:58.884
went smoothly. They passed the banks of Newfoundland without encountering fog or heavy

46
00:03:58.885 --> 00:04:05.761
seas, but on the third day, the clouds darkened and the wind blew from the southeast. To maintain his speed,

47
00:04:05.762 --> 00:04:10.843
Mr. Fogg instructed the crew to increase the fires in the

48
00:04:10.844 --> 00:04:15.289
engine's boilers. On December 16 the engineer, looking serious, came on deck and spoke

49
00:04:15.290 --> 00:04:23.079
with Mr. Fogg. "You are certain of what you tell me?" asked Mr. Fogg. "Absolutely," said the engineer. The engine's fires had been burning

50
00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:26.643
hot for too long.

51
00:04:26.644 --> 00:04:33.531
They were running out of coal. Mr. Fogg did not hesitate. "Feed the fires until the coal is exhausted," he said. The vessel continued

52
00:04:33.532 --> 00:04:37.141
at full speed, but three days away from Liverpool, the engineer announced that the coal

53
00:04:37.142 --> 00:04:44.771
was gone. Mr. Fogg asked Passepartout to bring Captain Speedy on deck. The captain appeared in a rage.

54
00:04:44.772 --> 00:04:48.022
"I have sent for you, sir—" began Mr. Fogg. "Pirate!" yelled

55
00:04:48.023 --> 00:04:52.848
Captain Speedy. Fogg continued, "—to ask you to sell me your ship." "By all the devils,

56
00:04:52.849 --> 00:04:59.288
no!" yelled the captain. "But I shall be obliged to burn her," said Mr. Fogg. Passepartout's Guidebook Mr. Fogg managed to find us

57
00:04:59.289 --> 00:05:10.220
a boat to Liverpool, though I didn't expect him to become the captain! Luckily, there were many boats

58
00:05:10.221 --> 00:05:14.153
traveling along the Hudson, one of the busiest rivers in

59
00:05:14.154 --> 00:05:19.221
the United States. The Hudson runs through New York State in the

60
00:05:19.222 --> 00:05:24.312
eastern part of the United States. It is 315 miles long and forms part of

61
00:05:24.313 --> 00:05:28.515
the boundary between the states of New York and New

62
00:05:28.516 --> 00:05:31.746
Jersey. The river is named after Henry Hudson, an Englishman

63
00:05:31.747 --> 00:05:35.369
who was sailing for the Dutch East India Company and

64
00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:40.739
explored it around 1609. Parts of the river are so beautiful that many

65
00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:45.978
artists have been inspired to paint it and the surrounding landscape. This art movement even

66
00:05:45.979 --> 00:05:50.731
has a name: the Hudson River School. As we rushed

67
00:05:50.732 --> 00:05:57.109
past, I would have liked to stop to paint the river too. But as you know, Mr. Fogg, was in

68
00:05:57.110 --> 00:06:04.581
quite a hurry to reach another body of water—the Atlantic Ocean!

